# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dependency staging plan for the 950PR x86_64 image.
#
# Every artefact that deps/950pr-x86_64/ must contain for
# Dockerfile.x86_64 to build with --network=none. targets/target-950pr/build.sh reads
# this file and refuses to start until each row is satisfied, so the plan is
# enforced rather than merely written down.
#
# Format:  kind | path relative to DEPS_DIR | bytes | sha256 | source
#   kind    file | dir | glob | pin
#   bytes   0 means "not size-checked" (directories, and sets whose size
#           depends on the resolve)
#   sha256  '-' means UNPINNED. The wheelhouse and apt rows stay unpinned
#           because their contents depend on the resolve.
#
# @CANN_VERSION@ AND @CANN_APT_VERSION@ ARE EXPANDED BY build.sh before this
# file is parsed, so one manifest covers every CANN line this target can build.
# The two are NOT interchangeable:
#
#   @CANN_VERSION@      the release LINE - 9.2.0. What CANN calls itself on disk.
#   @CANN_APT_VERSION@  the exact Debian version - 9.2.0-beta.2. What apt pinned,
#                       and therefore what the staged filenames carry.
#
# `pin` ROWS ARE VERSION-SCOPED INTEGRITY PINS and are the one place a literal
# version still appears - necessarily, since a hash is per artefact:
#
#   pin | <cann-apt-version> | <filename> | <bytes> | <sha256>
#
# build.sh applies only the rows whose version equals $CANN_APT_VERSION - the
# PACKAGE version, not the line, because 9.2.0-beta.1 and 9.2.0-beta.2 are
# different artefacts of the same line and share no hashes. A version with no
# pin row is size-checked but not hash-checked, with a warning; run
# `./targets/target-950pr/provision.sh cann`, which prints the sha256 of what it
# staged, and add the rows.
#
# WHERE CANN COMES FROM NOW (probed 2026-09-07):
#   DevCloud apt  https://ascend.devcloud.huaweicloud.com/cann/debian, suite
#                 `cann`, component `main`. GPG-signed; Huawei's cann-keyring
#                 package registers the key and the sources.list entry. Carries
#                 ascend-cann-{toolkit,nnal,950-ops,910-ops,910b-ops,310p-ops,
#                 310b-ops,A3-ops} from 8.5.0 through 9.2.0-beta.2, for amd64
#                 and arm64. THIS IS THE SOURCE.
#   OBS bucket    the previous source, and it never carried 9.2.0: 206 for
#                 9.0.0 and 9.1.0, 403 for every 9.2.0 spelling tried. Also had
#                 no Ascend-cann-kernels-950 at all, which is why this target
#                 used to lift operator libraries out of a vendor image.
#   quay.io       ascend/cann's 950 images stop at the 9.1.0 line, so there is
#                 no 9.2.0 vendor image to lift anything from - see cann_extra
#                 below, which is now optional for exactly that reason.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- CANN @CANN_APT_VERSION@, x86_64, from the DevCloud apt repository ------
# Downloaded by `provision.sh cann` with apt itself, so the repository's GPG
# signature is what authenticates them. The filenames are the ones apt gives a
# downloaded archive (<pkg>_<version>_<arch>.deb), which is NOT the name the
# file has on the repository.
#
# Each package wraps the vendor's own .run installer; the Dockerfile extracts
# and runs it rather than letting dpkg do it, because the postinst omits
# --quiet and stops on the EULA. See stage 3 of Dockerfile.x86_64.
dir|cann_debs|0|-|apt-get install --download-only inside ubuntu:22.04
file|cann_debs/ascend-cann-toolkit_@CANN_APT_VERSION@_amd64.deb|0|-|DevCloud apt repo, ascend-cann-toolkit=@CANN_APT_VERSION@
file|cann_debs/ascend-cann-950-ops_@CANN_APT_VERSION@_amd64.deb|0|-|DevCloud apt repo, ascend-cann-950-ops=@CANN_APT_VERSION@
file|cann_debs/ascend-cann-nnal_@CANN_APT_VERSION@_amd64.deb|0|-|DevCloud apt repo, ascend-cann-nnal=@CANN_APT_VERSION@

# Integrity pins, applied only for the matching CANN_APT_VERSION. These three
# are from the first verified fetch (2026-09-07) and each one was checked
# against the SHA256 the repository's own signed Packages index publishes for
# it, so the pin agrees with the vendor's index rather than merely with
# whatever arrived down the wire.
pin|9.2.0-beta.2|cann_debs/ascend-cann-toolkit_9.2.0-beta.2_amd64.deb|1395916760|d9b09d75248406750baff4f93f8bca05c4eeb2ba7cc26052191add73f130c4da
pin|9.2.0-beta.2|cann_debs/ascend-cann-950-ops_9.2.0-beta.2_amd64.deb|2834051262|c723b3aae658c6919277e77fbaaaacd4d64163892d701e53a0a40a52442473b7
pin|9.2.0-beta.2|cann_debs/ascend-cann-nnal_9.2.0-beta.2_amd64.deb|440036476|b917d8867508e550ffb6df7966a53d8b815b2a3afc0553940163a9404007689b

# --- cann_extra: the pre-apt workaround, now an escape hatch ---------------
# HISTORY, BECAUSE THE DIRECTORY STILL EXISTS AND ITS NAME EXPLAINS NOTHING.
# The standalone toolkit .run was an incomplete slice of a CANN install:
# measured on 9.1.0 its lib64 held 150 shared objects against the 176 in
# Huawei's own image for this SoC. Three of the missing twenty-six were
# load-bearing - libopapi.so (vllm-ascend links -lopapi for every SoC),
# libopapi_math.so (the compiled custom-ops package will not install without
# it) and libhccl.so (torch_npu's DT_NEEDED) - and NO published .run carried
# them, because Ascend-cann-kernels-950 returned 403 from the OBS bucket like
# everything else 950-shaped. They were lifted out of a vendor container's
# layer blob instead.
#
# THE FILE ROWS ARE GONE AND THE DIRECTORY MAY BE EMPTY. ascend-cann-950-ops
# is the operator package whose absence created this workaround; now that it is
# installable from the apt repository, the libraries it carries come from the
# release's own package instead of from a container image of a DIFFERENT
# release. cann_extra is kept as an escape hatch for a release whose ops package
# is missing something, and the Dockerfile copies whatever is here - but it
# asserts on the OUTCOME (libopapi.so present in the toolkit's lib64 after the
# ops install), not on the source, so an empty directory is a pass.
dir|cann_extra/lib64|0|-|optional; ascend-cann-950-ops normally supplies these

# --- vllm-ascend ACLNN third-party archives --------------------------------
# csrc/build_aclnn.sh builds 27 custom ops on the ascend950 branch and its
# CMake FetchContent-downloads these from gitcode.com unless they are already
# under ${CANN_3RD_LIB_PATH} (which defaults to csrc/third_party). makeself is
# NOT staged here - CANN ships its own copy and the Dockerfile copies that in.
dir|third_party/pkg|0|-|https://gitcode.com/cann-src-third-party
file|third_party/pkg/abseil-cpp-20230802.1.tar.gz|0|-|abseil-cpp/releases/download/20230802.1
file|third_party/pkg/protobuf-25.1.tar.gz|0|-|protobuf/releases/download/v25.1
file|third_party/json/include/nlohmann/json.hpp|0|-|json/releases/download/v3.11.3/include.zip

# --- Ubuntu 22.04 (jammy, amd64) local apt archive -------------------------
# The .deb closure of targets/target-950pr/packages/sys_packages.txt plus a
# dpkg-scanpackages index, built the way common/scripts/fetch_debs.sh does for the
# 310P but in a jammy container and with no QEMU in the way. Jammy because
# that is the base upstream's Dockerfile.a5 builds on; clang-15 resolves out
# of jammy-updates, which the stock ubuntu:22.04 image has enabled.
dir|apt_debs|0|-|apt-get install --download-only inside ubuntu:22.04
file|apt_debs/Packages.gz|0|-|dpkg-scanpackages . > Packages && gzip -k Packages

# --- cp310 / manylinux x86_64 wheelhouse -----------------------------------
# Resolved from targets/target-950pr/requirements/python_wheels.txt. cp310, not
# cp312, because the base is now Ubuntu 22.04 and jammy's system interpreter is
# python3.10; every pin in that file publishes a cp310 manylinux_2_28 x86_64
# wheel, and jammy's glibc 2.35 clears that floor.
#
# torch-npu 2.10.0.post4 is on PyPI: 2.10.0/.post2/.post4/.post6 ship as
# cp310-cp313 manylinux_2_28 wheels for x86_64 and aarch64. triton-ascend 3.2.2
# is the mirror-only one (PyPI stops at 3.2.0), and its index sits at the ROOT
# of the mirror path - a /simple/ prefix returns 404. Most likely staging
# failure; see docs/target-950pr-x86_64.md.
dir|python_wheels|0|-|pip download -r requirements/python_wheels.txt
glob|python_wheels/torch-2.10.0*.whl|0|-|https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
glob|python_wheels/torch_npu-2.10.0.post4*.whl|0|-|https://pypi.org/simple
glob|python_wheels/triton_ascend-3.2.2*.whl|0|-|https://mirrors.huaweicloud.com/ascend/repos/pypi

# The vLLM wheel is BUILT, not downloaded: PyPI's x86_64 vllm is a CUDA build.
# Build v0.27.1 from source with VLLM_TARGET_DEVICE=empty and drop the wheel
# here, exactly as the 310P pipeline does via common/scripts/build_vllm_wheel.sh.
glob|python_wheels/vllm-0.27.1*.whl|0|-|built from source, VLLM_TARGET_DEVICE=empty

# --- vllm-ascend source ----------------------------------------------------
# Built from source in stage 6. No patches are applied for this target.
# Staged from the codeload tarball rather than a git clone: the Dockerfile
# deletes .git anyway (setuptools-scm is fed VLLM_ASCEND_VERSION), and a
# depth-1 clone over the git protocol cannot resume a dropped connection.
#
# THE REF IS PINNED, and it is no longer `main`. main moves under you, so two
# provisioning runs a day apart produced different images from an unchanged
# repository - and the Dockerfile stamped a fixed SETUPTOOLS_SCM_PRETEND_VERSION
# on whatever arrived. provision.sh now fetches releases/v0.27.1rc by commit
# (VLLM_ASCEND_COMMIT), the branch upstream cuts against vLLM v0.27.1, which is
# already this target's VLLM_REF. src/vllm-ascend/.provenance records exactly
# what was staged.
file|src/vllm-ascend/setup.py|0|-|codeload vllm-ascend tar.gz at $VLLM_ASCEND_COMMIT
file|src/vllm-ascend/requirements.txt|0|-|same checkout
file|src/vllm-ascend/.provenance|0|-|written by provision.sh: repo, ref, commit, timestamp

# The catlass submodule, which a tarball does NOT carry - it ships the empty
# directory only. csrc/build_aclnn.sh takes the ascend950 branch, calls
# setup_catlass_dependency(), and runs `git submodule update` when
# csrc/third_party/catlass/include is absent; with --network=none that fails
# and takes the vllm-ascend wheel build with it. Staging include/ is enough,
# because the script skips the fetch when it is already there. The commit is
# pinned by vllm-ascend's own .gitmodules, not chosen here.
#
# THE PIN IS HONOURED BUT THE TREE IS PATCHED, ON CANN 9.2.0. .gitmodules pins
# 41bf90da655b, and that commit does not compile against 9.2.0-beta.2's Bisheng:
# the compiler now requires __attribute__((cce_simd_vf)) functions to be free or
# static, and catlass declares fourteen of them as non-static members. Three ops
# fail as a result - ChunkFwdO, ChunkGatedDeltaRuleFwdH, ChunkKdaFwd.
#
# Moving the pin forward to upstream's fix (c89fe73d) was tried and does NOT
# work: upstream deleted include/catlass/debug.hpp before that commit, and
# vllm-ascend at this ref still includes it, so no single catlass commit
# satisfies both. provision.sh therefore stages the pinned commit and applies
# targets/target-950pr/patches/0001-catlass-simd-vf-static.patch - c89fe73d's
# semantic change and nothing else. .provenance records the commit, what
# .gitmodules asked for, and which patch was applied.
dir|src/vllm-ascend/csrc/third_party/catlass/include|0|-|https://gitcode.com/cann/catlass.git @ .gitmodules commit, + the simd_vf backport
file|src/vllm-ascend/csrc/third_party/catlass/.provenance|0|-|written by provision.sh: url, commit, gitmodules_commit, patch
